<AnchorPane prefHeight="152.0" prefWidth="220.0" stylesheets="@../../../CSSstyles/MainLining.css" xmlns="http://javafx.com/javafx/23.0.1" xmlns:fx="http://javafx.com/fxml/1">
    <TextArea editable="false" prefHeight="152.0" prefWidth="220.0" stylesheets="@../../../CSSstyles/MainLining.css" text="Another name for this method of plant training is low-stress bending. It is one of the simplest ways to create a plant crown. The essence of the method is that the plant branches are located in a horizontal plane so that the distance from the lighting to the plant at any point is the same. Trim to three nodes Once your plant has formed 5-6 nodes, you can start pruning. To do this, you need to cut the stem just above the third pair of leaves. This will leave three nodes on the plant. You can take a cutting from the plant, and it will grow just like a clone! Cut off all the branches except the 3rd node. This will signal the plant to concentrate its energy on the two remaining branches. Do not remove the fan leaves. The fan leaves need to be left as they are responsible for the light coming in. After pruning, leave a couple of branches that you have. This will allow your plant to produce the sugar it needs to grow. Step 2.2 - Tie the two remaining branches to the base in the same way as lst does. Make sure they are almost at a 90 degree angle and that your plant is level. When pruning or training plants, remember that some may take longer to recover. Therefore, continue the work only after they have returned to normal. It is very important to create an ideal growing environment. If the temperature and humidity in the room are not optimal, the plants may not recover quickly. Make sure that the ties do not pull the branches together and check this regularly during the process. Step 3 - Once the plants have recovered, start feeding again. When you see that your plants have come to life and are blooming, it is time to prune again. Select opposite symmetrical nodes on each branch and feed them again. Pruning Your Plant for Maximum Yield After pruning, your plant will grow two branches, increasing the number of flower heads you will end up with. Next Step: Prune Below Do this immediately after pruning. Remove all growth below the cut, but be sure to keep the leaves associated with the newly created main cola." wrapText="true" />
</AnchorPane>
